    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C45-nosotros",
      "sequence": 1227,
      "title": "nosotros — the \"we\" the verb already says",
      "headword": "nosotros",
      "romanization": "nosotros",
      "gloss": "\"we\" — the word you have been leaving out since hablamos",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e8fad7d18f90bfe3",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"they love us.\" (Nos quieren.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nos is \"us.\" Today it becomes \"we\" — and you will find you have been saying \"we\" for a long time without it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nos is Latin nōs, which meant both we and us — the language had not yet split them into two words."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You have been saying \"we\" without it. The -mos ending on hablamos, comemos, tenemos already means we — it is the verb's own \"we\" ending, Latin -mus (amāmus, \"we love\"), a different word from nōs that happens to mean the same person. That is why hablamos alone is a whole sentence, and the pronoun is extra."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And the word for \"we\" itself:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nosotros — we, literally nos + otros, \"we others.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish added otros to the plain nos somewhere in the Middle Ages, to make clear the speaker meant this group and not the person being addressed. Catalan (nosaltres) and Occitan (nosautres) did the same; standard French and Italian did not, and in Spanish the seam is still visible in the word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"hablamos\" — \"we speak,\" with no pronoun at all",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"hablamos\" — \"we speak,\" with no pronoun at all]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"nosotros hablamos\" — \"we speak,\" said twice",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"nosotros hablamos\" — \"we speak,\" said twice]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "spoken": true,
              "id": "ES-C45-nosotros-ending",
              "prompt": "Which verb ending already says we, without nosotros?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-ETYMON-NOS-OTROS",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-AR-PRESENT-1PL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 10,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "-mos",
                "the -mos ending",
                "mos"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Hablamos says we on its own; nosotros is extra.",
                "incorrect": "The -mos of hablamos, from Latin -mus: the verb's own we ending."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "spoken": true,
              "id": "ES-C45-nosotros-word",
              "prompt": "What are the two pieces inside nosotros, and what do they mean together?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-NOSOTROS"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "nos and otros, we others",
                "nos + otros",
                "we others"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The seam is still visible.",
                "incorrect": "nos + otros, literally we others."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "spoken": true,
              "id": "ES-C45-nosotros-twice",
              "prompt": "Nosotros hablamos says we in two places. Name them.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-LEX-NOS-OBJECT",
                "ES-LEX-NOSOTROS"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "nosotros and the -mos ending",
                "nosotros and -mos",
                "the pronoun and the ending"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Twice in two words: the pronoun and the ending.",
                "incorrect": "The pronoun nosotros, and the -mos of hablamos."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is nosotros made of? (Nos + otros — \"we others.\") And which ending already says \"we\" without it? (-mos, from Latin -mus.) Next: the plural of lo and la, and you will not need to be told what they are."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
